# What code-review-graph should not index.
#
# Copy this to a project root as `.code-review-graphignore` BEFORE the first
# build — `graph-init` does it for you:
#
#     cp ~/claudebase/templates/project-code-review-graphignore .code-review-graphignore
#
# CRG keeps its OWN exclusion file, independent of .gitignore and of
# .graphifyignore. Excluding a path in either of those leaves CRG indexing it,
# so when you write one, write the sibling.
#
# Syntax is gitignore's, with one difference that decides how these rules are
# written (incremental.py:392-449). A bare `dir/` is rewritten to `**/dir/**`
# and matches at any depth. A plain glob is matched with fnmatch against the
# whole relative path, so `.*` would catch only *top-level* dotted entries and
# miss `sub/.obsidian/`. Hence the trailing slashes below.
#
# node_modules/, vendor/, venv/, .venv/, dist/, build/, target/, __pycache__
# and friends are already in DEFAULT_IGNORE_PATTERNS — they need no entry here.


# Editor and harness state. Not code anybody in the project wrote, and the
# biggest measured failure of the three graphs: one Obsidian vault tracked 367
# files of bundled plugin JS under .obsidian/, which produced 21,865 of 21,865
# nodes and a 222 MB graph.db describing somebody else's code. Nothing errored.
# Excluding it left 568 nodes — the honest size of what was authored by hand.
.obsidian/
.claude/
.omc/
.omp/
.omx/
.oms/
.omd/
.sp/

# The graphs' own output, for repos where it is tracked or where GRAPHIFY_OUT
# still points at the pre-.graphify default.
.graphify/
graphify-out/
.tokensave/

